ence of  clarity about that topic i mean we have  we have the howie test right but the  howie test was created back in 1946  um in a world pre-internet you know pre  it was like post right after world war  ii right so how could this howie test  apply to this brand new financial system  we're creating basically every founder  and investor and um  their highly paid lawyers are all kind  of trying to tease out like read the tea  leaves about what this could possibly  mean for their business and it's created  this kind of wide variety of  interpretations and a completely  ambiguous environment which kind of  stifles um startups and some of those  startups have decided to go abroad in to  incorporate because of the lack of  clarity in the u.s so now we're sitting  here in this environment  where um you know in the absence of that  clarity coinbase went out and sort of we  created things like the crypto rating  council we created a very thorough um  digital asset listing process internally  that looked at compliance and legal and  security cyber security and we were  actually pretty cautious about a lot of  the assets we added  um you know at the time that coinbase  had maybe 50 assets or so there was  uh some of our competitors had hundreds  of them right and now we have about 100  assets total that we've gotten  comfortable with many of them other ones  we've rejected and so i would expect um  this whole environment is just over the  next year or so it's going to be  a series of enforcement actions a series  of behind closed door discussions legal  precedent getting created um there will  probably be broker dealer licenses  incorporated um or i should say  operationalized you know coinbase went  out and got its brokerage license a  while back but we haven't  operationalized it because there aren't  any security tokens that our customers  want to to actually trade and so maybe  once we get that operational then other  crypto tokens will then go be pushed to  register with the sec  we actually want to make that easy as  well through a product we have called  coinbase asset hub and just make it  super simple for any asset issuer to  come you know  form a smart smart contract or however  they want to do it create a new a new  asset register it with  the sec if they need to do that in the  us do a reg s offering if they want to  do it abroad  so we want to try to make all this  easier  more regulated i think we're all pretty  much aligned even the regulators and and  the industry there's just going to be a  bunch of  silly headlines and you know  uncomfortable conversations as that all  plays out  so i i want to try to kind of summarize  what i believe to be ural's position but  if i misspeak uh correct me is basically  look we want to play within the rules we  think regulation is generally good uh  but in some areas we don't know what the  rules are and so we're trying our best  but if we don't know what those rules  are and then all of a sudden we get told  hey don't do that it's kind of hard to  operate a business with uncertainty uh  it feels like that's one kind of talk  track or uh um you know viewpoint  another is hey there's things that we  think should be allowed that feels like  the regulators are telling us they  aren't allowed and we want to have a  discussion and maybe there isn't a  discussion at the level that you want to  have it or uh you're not getting  guidance that you're looking for etc and  then there's like this third world which  is basically like hey we do things as a  publicly traded company in a regulated  environment in the united states but  like look globally and there's people  doing all kinds of different things and  so there's like a global competition  going on how much does the us want to  over regulate or regulate versus like  wide open free markets and just let  everyone go let the free market decide  is that kind of a fair characterization  of like maybe like the three points  around regulation and